"# Experiment Notebooks\n\nThis directory contains the experimental notebooks developed for the final-year project:\n\n> **A Cross-Domain Study of Accuracy, Calibration, and Robustness in CNN, Transformer, and Sequential Vision Models**\n\nThe notebooks implement experiments for evaluating vision models across different visual domains. The study goes beyond classification accuracy by also examining confidence calibration and robustness under corrupted inputs.\n\n---\n\n## Project Scope\n\nThe overall project evaluates three vision model architectures across three datasets representing different visual domains:\n\n| Dataset | Domain | Models |\n|---|---|---|\n| CIFAR-100 | Natural Images | ResNet-18, ViT-Small, Vision Mamba |\n| EuroSAT | Satellite Imagery | ResNet-18, ViT-Small, Vision Mamba |\n| Brain MRI | Medical Imaging | ResNet-18, ViT-Small, Vision Mamba |\n\nThe Brain MRI experiment notebook is not currently included in this directory and will be added when the corresponding source implementation is available.\n\n---\n\n## Available Notebooks\n\n### CIFAR-100\n\n**Notebook:** `new-cifar-100.ipynb`\n\nThis notebook contains the CIFAR-100 experiments for comparing:\n\n- ResNet-18\n- ViT-Small\n- Vision Mamba\n\nThe experiments include model training and evaluation, calibration analysis, robustness testing, repeated-seed experiments, statistical analysis, and result visualization.\n\nCIFAR-100 represents the natural-image classification domain in the project.\n\n---\n\n### EuroSAT\n\n**Notebook:** `new-eurostat.ipynb`\n\nThis notebook contains the EuroSAT experiments for comparing:\n\n- ResNet-18\n- ViT-Small\n- Vision Mamba\n\nThe experiments follow the project's evaluation framework for satellite imagery, including model evaluation, calibration analysis, robustness testing, repeated-seed experiments, statistical analysis, and result visualization.\n\nEuroSAT represents the remote-sensing/satellite-imagery domain in the project.\n\n---\n\n### Brain MRI\n\n**Notebook:** Not yet added\n\nBrain MRI represents the medical-imaging domain in the overall study.\n\nThe corresponding notebook will be added once the source implementation is available.\n\n---\n\n## Models\n\nThe project compares three different vision architecture families.\n\n### ResNet-18\n\nResNet-18 is used as the convolutional neural network (CNN) model in the study.\n\nThe project uses pretrained ResNet-18 weights and adapts the classification head to the requirements of each dataset.\n\n### ViT-Small\n\nViT-Small is used as the transformer-based vision model.\n\nThe project uses pretrained weights and adapts the model for the classification tasks.\n\n### Vision Mamba\n\nVision Mamba represents the sequential/state-space model in the study.\n\nThe project uses Mamba-based sequential blocks operating on image patch tokens, with the Vision Mamba model trained from scratch.\n\n---\n\n## Experimental Pipeline\n\nThe project follows a common experimental workflow across datasets and architectures:\n\n```text\nDataset\n   │\n   ▼\nData Preparation\n   │\n   ▼\nPreprocessing\n   │\n   ▼\nModel Training\n   │\n   ▼\nTest Evaluation\n   │\n   ▼\nCalibration Analysis\n   │\n   ▼\nRobustness Testing\n   │\n   ▼\nStatistical Analysis\n   │\n   ▼\nResult Aggregation & Visualization\n```\n\nThe project was designed so that the same evaluation logic could be applied across datasets and model architectures, helping maintain consistency in the comparison. \n\n---\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n\nThe experiments evaluate models using multiple aspects of performance.\n\n### Classification Performance\n\nThe project evaluates:\n\n- Accuracy\n- F1 Score\n\nThe implementation also reports additional classification statistics such as precision and recall where applicable.\n\n### Confidence Calibration\n\nModel confidence is evaluated using:\n\n- Expected Calibration Error (ECE)\n- Maximum Calibration Error (MCE)\n- Temperature Scaling\n\nTemperature scaling is fitted using the validation set and then evaluated on the held-out test set. This maintains the separation between validation and test data described in the project methodology. \n\n### Robustness\n\nRobustness is evaluated by applying image corruptions to test inputs.\n\nThe project considers:\n\n- Gaussian noise\n- Gaussian blur\n\nMultiple corruption strengths are used to examine how model performance changes as the input becomes more degraded. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\n\n---\n\n## Repeated Experiments\n\nThe project uses five random seeds for repeated experiments.\n\n```text\n[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\n```\n\nRepeating experiments across multiple seeds helps reduce the influence of random initialization, minibatch ordering, and other sources of training variation.\n\nThe project reports aggregated results using mean and standard deviation across seeds. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\n\n---\n\n## Training Setup\n\nThe project uses a common training framework across the experiments.\n\nThe methodology described in the project report includes:\n\n- AdamW optimization\n- Learning-rate scheduling\n- Early stopping\n- Mixed-precision acceleration where available\n- Consistent dataset splits\n- Multiple random seeds\n\nResNet-18 and ViT-Small use pretrained weights, while Vision Mamba is trained from scratch. \n\n---\n\n## Statistical Analysis\n\nThe experimental results are analyzed across the repeated runs to examine differences between model architectures.\n\nThe project also uses statistical analysis to support comparisons across models rather than relying only on individual best-performing runs.\n\nThe resulting summaries are used alongside plots and tables for interpretation.\n\n---\n\n## Outputs\n\nThe experimental notebooks are used to produce and analyze outputs including:\n\n- Classification performance results\n- Calibration measurements\n- Robustness measurements\n- Reliability diagrams\n- Performance visualizations\n- Calibration visualizations\n- Noise robustness analysis\n- Blur robustness analysis\n- Temperature-scaling analysis\n- Summary tables\n- Structured CSV results\n- Model checkpoints\n\nThe project report describes the generation of structured CSV outputs to support later aggregation and analysis across datasets. \n\n---\n\n## Dataset Preparation\n\nThe overall project uses a consistent data preparation approach while adapting preprocessing to the characteristics of each dataset.\n\n### CIFAR-100\n\nCIFAR-100 is used as the natural-image benchmark.\n\nThe project report describes resizing, normalization, and standard augmentation such as random cropping and horizontal flipping for the CIFAR-100 experiments. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\n\nNotebook:\n\n```text\nnew-cifar-100.ipynb\n```\n\n### EuroSAT\n\nEuroSAT is used as the satellite-imagery dataset.\n\nIt contains images representing 10 land-use classes derived from Sentinel-2 satellite data. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\n\nNotebook:\n\n```text\nnew-eurostat.ipynb\n```\n\n### Brain MRI\n\nBrain MRI is used as the medical-imaging dataset and is prepared as a binary classification task in the overall project. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\n\nThe corresponding experiment notebook will be added later.\n\n---\n\n## Reproducibility\n\nThe experiments were executed in GPU-based Kaggle environments.\n\nThe project was designed around:\n\n- Fixed random seeds\n- Consistent dataset splits\n- A common evaluation framework\n- Structured experiment outputs\n- Separation of validation and test data\n\nThe project report identifies Kaggle GPU environments as an implementation constraint and notes the use of Python, PyTorch, torchvision, timm, NumPy, pandas, matplotlib, and SciPy in the experimental environment. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\n\nThe current notebooks may contain environment-specific paths or configurations from the original experimental environment. These may need to be adapted when running the notebooks elsewhere.\n\nFor general environment setup, see:\n\n[`Setup Guide`](../docs/setup.md)\n\nThe datasets themselves are not included in this repository.\n\n---\n\n## Project Documentation\n\nThe complete project methodology, implementation details, experimental design, and findings are documented in the project report.\n\nThe project presentation provides a visual overview of the study and its results.\n\nBoth documents are maintained in the [`docs`](../docs/) directory.\n\n---\n\n## Repository Development\n\nThe current notebooks preserve the experimental implementations developed during the project.\n\nAs the repository is organized further, reusable components may be separated from the notebooks into dedicated Python modules.\n\nPotential reusable components include:\n\n- Dataset preparation\n- Model definitions\n- Training utilities\n- Evaluation metrics\n- Calibration methods\n- Robustness evaluation\n- Statistical analysis\n- Result aggregation\n\nThe goal is to keep the notebooks focused on experiment configuration, execution, and analysis while allowing reusable functionality to be maintained independently.\n\n---\n\n## Research Context\n\nThe central motivation of the project is that classification accuracy alone does not fully describe the reliability of a vision model.\n\nThe study therefore considers three complementary aspects:\n\n```text\nPerformance\n     +\nCalibration\n     +\nRobustness\n```\n\nThe final analysis compares how the relative behaviour of CNN, transformer, and sequential vision architectures changes across different visual domains and evaluation criteria. \n"